  "status": "published",
  "title": "Chicken Coop for Four",
  "summary": "A raised coop with nesting boxes, a clean-out door and a predator-proof run.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 30,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Pick a level, well-drained spot and mark a 4 x 12 ft footprint: a 4 x 4 ft raised coop at one end and a 4 x 8 ft run attached to it. Strip the sod inside the marks.",
    "Cut the two 4x4 posts into four 48 in legs. Build a 4 x 4 ft floor frame from 2x4s and screw it to the legs 24 in above the ground, pre-drilling near every board end. Check it for level and square by measuring both diagonals.",
    "Deck the floor with plywood. Frame the walls from 2x4s on 24 in centres, 48 in tall at the front and 36 in at the back so the roof slopes away from the run.",
    "Sheath the walls with plywood. Cut a 12 x 12 in pop door into the run, a 24 x 36 in clean-out door at the back, and vent slots high under the eaves. Cover each vent with hardware cloth on the inside.",
    "Build two 12 in cube nesting boxes from plywood offcuts, cantilevered off one side wall with a hinged lid for egg collection. Screw a 2x4 roost bar, wide face up, 18 in above the floor and higher than the nest boxes so the hens do not sleep in them.",
    "Frame the 4 x 8 ft run from 2x4s, 48 in tall, with a hinged access door. Wrap the walls and the top in hardware cloth, fixing it every 6 in with 1 1/4 in screws through fender washers. Chicken wire keeps chickens in but does not keep raccoons out, which is why this build uses hardware cloth.",
    "Lay a 12 in apron of hardware cloth flat on the ground around the outside of the run, screw it to the bottom rail, and cover it with the sod you stripped. Diggers hit the mesh and give up.",
    "Roof the coop with corrugated steel panels, overhanging 3 in on every side, fixed with the neoprene-washer roofing screws in the pattern the panel maker specifies.",
    "Hang the doors on T-hinges. Fit barrel bolts and clip a carabiner through each bolt, because raccoons can open a latch that needs only one motion.",
    "Stain every outside surface and let it cure fully before the birds move in. Then add 3 in of pine shavings to the coop floor."
